      "timepoint": "over 12 months after adding entecavir",
      "effect": "rapid and continuous suppression",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "virological success",
      "timepoint": "over 12 months",
      "effect": "achieved",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "HIV-RNA suppression",
      "timepoint": "during previous tenofovir/lamivudine therapy",
      "effect": "constantly suppressed",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": [
    {
      "event": "treatment-emergent adverse events",
      "rate": "none reported; treatment well-tolerated and safe"
    }
  ],
  "other_facts": "No known mutations associated with tenofovir resistance (rtA181T/V, rtN236T, A194T) were detected. A unique HBV substitution pattern with rtR192PR mutation at the time of virological failure was found. The rtR192PR mutation is located in the B domain near rtA194T, occurring in a context of very complex substitution patterns, and might be associated with resistance to tenofovir.",
  "summary": "A case report of a 22-year-old HBV/HIV co-infected patient with lamivudine and tenofovir resistance, where adding entecavir 1 mg daily resulted in HBV-DNA suppression for over 12 months, with a novel rtR192PR mutation potentially associated with tenofovir resistance."
}

