  "other_facts": "Mn(III) N-alkyl- and N-alkoxyalkylpyridyporphyrins (MnPs) protect normal tissue and suppress tumor growth via modulation of NF-κB and Nrf2 pathways through catalysis of MnP/H2O2-driven protein thiol oxidation. BMX-001 is the most clinically advanced Mn porphyrin, having progressed into five Phase II clinical trials, two for CNS injuries. No efficient treatment for chemotherapy-induced neuropathy is available. In the study, female CD-1 mice were evaluated for body weight, muscular coordination, and general physical condition (rotarod) to assess toxicity. BMX-001 suppressed peripheral neuropathy and neuroinflammation while protecting peripheral tissue. In a CAOV2 high-grade serous ovarian cancer subcutaneous xenograft model, BMX-001 as a single agent suppressed tumor growth, comparable to PTX. Previously, strong anticancer effect required combination with radiation, chemotherapy, and ascorbate. High-grade serous ovarian cancer is the first in vivo cancer where Mn porphyrin shows strong single-agent anticancer effect, likely due to high tumor levels of BMX-001 and high oxidative stress in chemoresistant CAOV2 cells. This could allow lower PTX dosing and reduce neuropathy. Combined neuropathy protection and anticancer efficacy show therapeutic potential for gynecological cancers. BMX-001 may suppress neuropathy relevant for all cancers using neurotoxic chemotherapeutics.",
