  "other_facts": "Admission: September 2000. Diagnosis: systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) with lupus nephritis WHO class V. Initial treatment: methylprednisolone (m-PSL) pulse therapy followed by oral prednisolone (PSL). Disease activity decreased after initial treatment. While tapering PSL from 60 to 30 mg/day, lupus nephritis flared up in February 2001. Re-hospitalized in February 2001. Retreatment: m-PSL pulse therapy followed by tapering from 60 to 30 mg/day. Mizoribine (MZR) added as combination therapy after tapering to 30 mg/day. Lupus nephritis flared again after tapering PSL to 17.5 mg/day. MZR dosage changed from 150 mg/day in three divided doses to 200 mg/day in two divided doses. Peak blood concentration (Cmax) of MZR increased from 0.63 to 1.55 microg/ml after dose modification. Oral PSL successfully tapered to 7.5 mg/day. Remission achieved without any side effects. Monitoring of serum concentration of MZR considered important for effective therapy, especially for steroid-resistant lupus nephritis. If serum MZR concentration does not reach effective level, adjust dosage to maintain adequate concentration.",
  "summary": "A 29-year-old male with systemic lupus erythematosus and lupus nephritis achieved remission after dose adjustment of mizoribine based on serum concentration monitoring, enabling tapering of prednisolone to 7.5 mg/day without side effects."
