  "other_facts": "Questionnaires distributed to 57 senior physiotherapists across all thoracic surgical units in Australia and New Zealand; response rate 81% (46/57). Pre-operatively, 35% (16/46) assessed all thoracotomy patients. Post-operatively, 96% (44/46) indicated all patients were seen by physiotherapists; 63% (29/46) performed prophylactic physiotherapy to prevent pulmonary complications. Physiotherapy treatment usually commenced on day one post-operatively in 80% (37/46). Most commonly used interventions: deep breathing exercises, active cycle of breathing techniques, cough, forced expiration techniques, sustained maximal inspirations. First sat out of bed on day one: 89% (41/46); commenced shoulder range of movement on day one: 50% (23/46); walking on day one: 70% (32/46). No post-operative pulmonary rehabilitation offered by 54% (25/46); no outpatient follow-up by 94% (43/46); no post-thoracotomy pain management by 87% (40/46). Factors influencing practice: personal experience, literature recommendations, established practice.",
  "summary": "A survey of physiotherapy management in Australian and New Zealand thoracic units found that most thoracotomy patients receive post-operative physiotherapy starting day one, with deep breathing and airway clearance techniques being most common, but pre-operative assessment and post-discharge follow-up are limited."
}

