  "other_facts": "New insights into biochemistry of cardiac arrhythmia and skeletal muscle fatigue have yielded new drug candidates targeting ryanodine receptor (RyR)-based Ca2+-release channels that leak under strenuous exercise, causing aberrant calcium sparks. Therapeutics called rycals are being developed to treat cardiac arrhythmia via enhancement of calstabin-ryanodine affinities, stabilizing RyR. These therapeutics possess potential for misuse in sports. Early implementation of target analytes such as benzothiazepine derivatives S-107 and JTV-519 or putative metabolites into doping control screening procedures is recommended. Reference compounds, deuterated analogues, and a putative metabolic product were synthesized; electron ionization mass spectra were studied and dissociation pathways elucidated by tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS) and accurate mass measurements. The characterized analytes were incorporated into existing sports drug testing assays based on liquid-liquid extraction and subsequent gas chromatography/mass spectrometry (GC/MS) analysis. The established procedure proved suitable for routine doping control analysis to detect potential misuse of S-107 in elite sport.",
  "summary": "An analytical method for detecting the ryanodine receptor stabilizer S-107 in doping control was developed and validated, demonstrating suitability for routine screening with a limit of detection of 4-6 ng/mL, precision of 1.5-17.2%, and recovery of 63-66%."
}

