  "other_facts": "Objective: to determine feasibility and usefulness of collecting 9 previously described quality indicators of CVD prevention in primary care. Setting: family health team in Hamilton, Ont, comprising approximately 30,000 patients and 25 physicians over 2 sites. Results were adjusted for age, sex, and family health team site. Obesity measurements were collected more frequently in women, while blood pressure measurements and follow-up were completed more frequently in men. The collection of these indicators was significantly associated with behavioural changes related to CVD prevention. Collection of the indicators does not generally predict short-term outcomes; however, collection of most indicators increased the odds of patient or physician behavioural changes for the primary prevention of CVD.",
