  "other_facts": "Heparin is a widely applied anticoagulant. Silicon nanoparticles (SiNPs) modified by amino groups and glutathione-capped gold nanoclusters (GSH-AuNCs) self-assemble into spherical particles via electrostatic interaction, causing aggregation-enhanced emission (AEE) of GSH-AuNCs. Heparin, a highly sulfated glycosaminoglycan with many negative charges, binds SiNPs and inhibits aggregation, quenching AEE at 570 nm while SiNPs fluoresce at 450 nm. SiNPs act as internal reference, GSH-AuNCs as signal probe. Ratiometric fluorescent signal (I570/I450) positively correlates with heparin concentrations in the range 6.44 ng/mL to 96.6 ng/mL. Detection limit is 3.29 ng/mL. The method shows good sensitivity and selectivity, and was successfully applied to detect heparin in heparin sodium injection and human serum samples with good recoveries.",
  "summary": "A ratiometric fluorescent nanoprobe using SiNPs and GSH-AuNCs detects heparin in the range 6.44–96.6 ng/mL with a detection limit of 3.29 ng/mL, showing good sensitivity and selectivity in injection and serum samples."
}

