  "other_facts": "Visual changes due to hyperglycemia in diabetes are not uncommon; blurred vision is a well-established sequela of chronic hyperglycemia. Homonymous hemianopia with or without electroclinical seizures is much rarer and can be mistaken for migraine, temporal arteritis, or ischemia. Patients (67M, 68M, 52F) presented with complex visual phenomena lasting 3 to 42 days. Examinations showed dense left homonymous hemianopias in 2 patients and left inferior homonymous quadrantanopia in one, with no other abnormalities. Vivid, nonstereotyped intermittent hallucinations occurred in the affected fields. CT brain showed no acute intracranial pathology; MRI either detected no abnormalities or demonstrated changes consistent with seizure activity. EEG showed seizures over the right occipital region in each patient, coinciding with hallucinations while they remained conscious. Rapid and complete reversal of seizures and visual field deficits after treatment was confirmed by repeat Automated 30-2 and MRI. Hyperglycemia-induced occipital lobe seizures with visual hallucinations and interictal homonymous visual field defects are a rare but clinically important diagnosis requiring prompt recognition and treatment.",
  "summary": "This case series of 3 patients with hyperglycemia-induced occipital lobe seizures presenting with visual hallucinations and homonymous visual field defects highlights the importance of prompt recognition and treatment with oral hypoglycemic and antiepileptic medications for rapid recovery."
